WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.049
Scenario: A supplier sends an invoice document you were not expecting.

2
00:00:04.049 --> 00:00:07.362
When opened in a preview, it asks you to

3
00:00:07.362 --> 00:00:10.675
“Enable Content” to display the invoice. Safest response: Option

4
00:00:10.675 --> 00:00:13.988
B: Verify the invoice with the supplier through known

5
00:00:13.988 --> 00:00:18.037
contact details and report the suspicious request; do not enable content.

6
00:00:18.037 --> 00:00:22.086
Unexpected documents and requests to enable active content require independent verification.

7
00:00:22.086 --> 00:00:25.399
Option A is unsafe because Disconnecting does not make

8
00:00:25.399 --> 00:00:28.712
untrusted active content safe; it may still alter or

9
00:00:28.712 --> 00:00:32.025
persist on the device. Option C is unsafe because

10
00:00:32.025 --> 00:00:35.337
Uploading a business document publicly may disclose confidential data;

11
00:00:35.337 --> 00:00:38.650
use organization-approved analysis channels. Option D is unsafe because

12
00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:41.963
Unexpected active content can execute code and should not

13
00:00:41.963 --> 00:00:45.276
be enabled simply to view a document. In practice:

14
00:00:45.276 --> 00:00:48.589
Close the document without enabling content. Verify with the

15
00:00:48.589 --> 00:00:51.902
supplier using a known contact method and use approved

16
00:00:51.902 --> 00:00:55.215
security analysis if needed. Key takeaway: Do not enable

17
00:00:55.215 --> 00:00:58.528
macros or active content in an unexpected document to

18
00:00:58.528 --> 00:01:00.000
